\subsection{The asymmetry: the full factorial picture}
\label{sec:asymmetry}

Table~\ref{tab:factorial} reports every combination of image and context polarity. Our primary comparison is between the two conflict conditions. A negative sentence moves positive-image judgments by $-0.598$. The reverse conflict, a positive sentence on negative images, moves judgments by $+0.333$. We call the difference between these magnitudes the \emph{mirror contrast}; it is $+0.265$ (bootstrap 95\% CI $[+0.095, +0.431]$; Mann-Whitney $p = 0.005$), or about a $1.8\times$ ratio.

Negative images begin closer to the lower bound of the score, so they have less room to move. We account for this by dividing each shift by the available room in that direction. The negative and positive shifts remain unequal after this adjustment ($0.632$ vs.\ $0.259$), so the result is not caused by the score's lower bound. The categorical result is similar: negative context overrides a positive image on $84\%$ of trials, whereas positive context overrides a negative image on $21\%$. The gap is $+64\%$, with a 95\% CI clustered over images of $[+55, +72]$.

A second comparison uses only positive images, whose neutral baseline sits near the middle of the score range ($-0.058$). On these images, negative and positive contexts differ by $3.58\times$ in their distance from neutral ($-0.598$ vs.\ $+0.167$). This ratio compares a conflicting context with an \emph{agreeing} context on the same image; it is not the mirror contrast above. We therefore report it separately. On its own, the ratio can be misleading: \llava{} reaches $3.06\times$ because of the score's bounds even though its mirror contrast is null (\S\ref{sec:models}).

