\subsection{Valence, not event semantics: token-matched minimal pairs}
\label{sec:minimal}

Could the asymmetry come from the sentences rather than their valence? Without an image, the positive and negative banks have similar mean strength ($+0.706$ vs.\ $-0.752$). With only six sentences per polarity, however, a non-significant test cannot establish that the banks are equivalent. A stronger concern remains: ``funeral'' and ``championship'' describe different events, and some events may point more directly to particular emotion labels.

We therefore built six \emph{minimal pairs}. Each pair uses the same sentence and changes only one valence-bearing word: won$\leftrightarrow$lost, best$\leftrightarrow$worst, got$\leftrightarrow$lost, wonderful$\leftrightarrow$devastating, celebration$\leftrightarrow$memorial, or joyful$\leftrightarrow$heartbreaking. Each pair also has the same token count under the Gemma tokenizer, so its readout position does not change.

