\paragraph{The image creates the asymmetry.}
Without an image, the positive and negative banks have similar effects. With a positive image present, only $24\%$ of the positive-text effect remains, compared with $80\%$ of the negative-text effect. In other words, the image suppresses the text that agrees with it much more than the text that conflicts with it. This makes the asymmetry a cross-modal pattern rather than a property of the sentences alone. We treat this as a strong descriptive interaction. A formal test that accounts for variation across both images and sentences remains future work (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

