\qwen{} closely replicates the asymmetry. Its graded valence score reaches the bounds of the scale, so we rely primarily on the override rate for the cross-model comparison; its mirror contrast is $+0.394$. \llava{} shows a different pattern. Its judgments remain anchored to the image, with neutral-context valence near $\pm 0.72$. The two conflict directions have equal adjusted shifts ($0.33/0.33$), the mirror-contrast CI includes zero, and the override gap is slightly reversed ($-13\%$).

One possible explanation is that LLaVA's unpooled frozen-CLIP input gives the image enough weight to resist textual override in both directions. We have not tested that explanation directly. The evidence supports a narrower conclusion: the asymmetry appears in the pooled Gemma and native-ViT Qwen designs, but not in the linear-projector LLaVA design. It is therefore not universal across VLMs. A coarse version of the patching result also replicates on \qwen{}: image tokens recover none of the context difference, while all text positions together recover $65\%$. Unlike Gemma, Qwen spreads the information across several text positions rather than concentrating it at the turn boundary. We did not run mechanism experiments on \llava{}, where there is no negative-text dominance to explain.

\subsection{Prompt robustness}
\label{sec:prompts}

Rephrasing the question six natural ways with everything else fixed (13{,}500 forwards), the override gap never flips and every CI clears zero: $+46\%$ to $+74\%$, mean $+58\%$, with the base prompt mid-pack (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}). The sweep reuses the same context bank, so it establishes robustness to phrasing, not to context sampling.

\subsection{Scale}
\label{sec:scale}

From \gemma{} to \gemmabig{}, the gap remains large ($+51\%$, CI $[+0.42, +0.59]$). Negative override rises ($84\% \to 93\%$) and positive override more than doubles ($19\% \to 42\%$): the larger model is more text-driven in \emph{both} directions. Because the two gap CIs overlap and the negative rate is near ceiling at $93\%$, we do not claim the asymmetry itself changes with scale (Appendix~\ref{app:scale}).

\subsection{Steering causally shifts conflict outcomes}
\label{sec:arbitration}

Finally, we test whether this activation space can causally change a judgment during conflict. Injecting the text-derived pleasantness direction at layer 18 moves behavioral valence with a slope of $+0.215$ per unit of steering strength. This is about $65\%$ of the no-conflict slope ($+0.329$). A direction learned only from text therefore changes the model's output while image and text disagree. This establishes causal control of the outcome, but not necessarily of the competition between the cues. A general output-valence direction could shift the final score without changing either cue's weight. Testing whether steering changes override rates or cue coefficients is the next step.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Where the cues meet.}
The patching experiments point to the same downstream text-token states for both cues. Same-image patching shows that these states carry the context difference. Cross-image patching shows that visual valence becomes recoverable from them over depth. These findings identify a plausible meeting point for image-text conflict. They do not show why negative context has the larger effect. The pair-level results offer one clue: negative framing is consistent, while positive framing depends more on the event. Patching neutral-to-positive and neutral-to-negative changes separately would test whether the asymmetry arises while information is being carried through the network or is already present in the inputs.

\paragraph{Why would LLaVA differ?}
The LLaVA result rules out a simple explanation based only on negative language being more informative. If that were sufficient, LLaVA should show the asymmetry too. A plausible alternative is that the vision-language design controls how much influence the image retains. LLaVA's unpooled projection may keep the image strong enough to resist text in both directions, whereas the Gemma and Qwen designs allow more textual override. This is a hypothesis, not a tested mechanism. Intermediate designs such as LLaVA-NeXT and OneVision could test it.

\paragraph{Conflicts with stored model knowledge.}
In conflicts between an image and knowledge stored in the model's weights, prior work finds that image tokens carry most of the causal effect \citep{nooralahzadeh2026, lietzow2026}. In our conflicts, the competing text is part of the prompt, and its effect travels through text-token states. Even the image signal becomes recoverable from text positions by the readout. Where a conflict is resolved therefore depends on the type of conflict, the layer, and the model design.

\paragraph{Implications.}
On the affect-sensitive task studied here, a single negative sentence overrides the visual judgment of Gemma and Qwen at rates near $90\%$. Textual influence also grows with model scale, although the asymmetry itself does not clearly grow. Model choice matters: LLaVA behaves qualitatively differently under the same conflicts. For Gemma-like models, the states near the start of the answer are a concrete place to monitor, since they carry the context difference and can be steered to change the output.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{Mechanism.} Patching shows where the context difference travels, but not why negative context has the larger effect. We have not separately patched neutral-to-positive and neutral-to-negative changes. Near-zero recovery from image tokens is also partly expected because the image appears before the context. We have not tested the reverse order. Finally, the tokens before the answer may be a general place where prompt information gathers; a matched non-affective control is needed to test whether their role is specific to valence. The full-bank patching sentences differ in token length, so we also plan to repeat the experiment with the token-matched pairs. Under fixed negative context, the image-driven gap becomes too small to localize its path reliably.

\textbf{Statistics.} Confidence intervals account for variation over 121 distinct images, but not variation over images and context sentences at the same time. A two-way bootstrap over both sources is future work. The minimal-pair within-item test partly addresses this issue, but is marginal ($p = 0.052$). Two positive members of the minimal pairs also have weak effects without an image and should be revised. One image appears in both polarity groups; removing it changes the headline contrast by only $0.018$. All runs use a single seed.

\textbf{Measurement.} Behavioral valence is a probability score bounded between $-1$ and $1$. We account for the available room on the scale and also report an override rate, but we have not repeated the analysis with an unbounded logit-margin score. EMOTIC labels people within images, whereas we provide the whole image without marking the target person. A rerun with marked people or cropped images is the direct fix.

\textbf{Scope.} We study one dataset, one task family (person-emotion labeling), one context style, and three model designs. The mechanism analysis is detailed for one model and coarse for a second. Our claims therefore concern controlled image-context emotion judgments in the tested models. Other conflict domains, tasks, and architectures remain open.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Cross-modal conflict resolution is valence-asymmetric in two of the three VLM designs we test: negative language changes image-grounded emotion judgments much more than positive language does. The result survives token-matched minimal pairs and appears only when an image is present. It holds in Gemma-3 and Qwen3-VL but not in image-anchored LLaVA-1.5. In Gemma, patching shows that downstream text-token states can carry valence information from both the context and the image. A direction learned only from text can then be used at those states to causally shift the output during conflict. Multimodal conflict therefore depends not only on which modalities disagree, but also on the valence of their evidence and on how the model combines vision with language.

